      "updated": 1784655845088,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- REST API design\n  - Model resources (nouns), not actions — /orders/{id}/cancel, not /cancelOrder\n  - Use HTTP verbs/status codes correctly: 200/201/204 success, 400 validation, 401/403 auth, 404 missing, 409 conflict, 422 semantic error, 5xx server fault\n- API versioning: URI (/v1/) simplest for public APIs; header/media-type versioning for stricter contract evolution — never break existing clients silently\n- Pagination: cursor-based for large/real-time-changing datasets (stable under inserts/deletes); offset/limit fine for small, static, admin-facing lists\n- Idempotency: PUT/DELETE must be idempotent by definition; for POST (payments, order creation) require an Idempotency-Key header so retries don't double-create\n- Auth patterns\n  - Session + cookie: simplest, server holds state, easy revocation — good default for first-party web apps\n  - JWT: stateless, scales horizontally, but hard to revoke before expiry — keep access tokens short-lived + refresh tokens\n  - OAuth2/OIDC: delegate auth to a provider (Google/Auth0) for third-party or enterprise SSO — don't roll your own\n- ORM vs raw SQL: ORM (SQLAlchemy/Django ORM) for velocity and safety on CRUD-heavy paths; drop to raw SQL/query builder for reporting, bulk ops, or anywhere the ORM generates pathological queries\n- Migrations: every schema change goes through a versioned migration (Alembic/Django migrations), forwards AND backwards compatible during rolling deploy — never hand-edit the prod schema\n- Connection pooling: reuse a bounded pool (pgbouncer, SQLAlchemy pool) sized to DB max_connections / (num app instances) — unbounded connections are a classic outage cause\n- N+1 queries: the #1 ORM footgun — fix with eager loading (select_related/joinedload, prefetch_related/selectinload); watch for it in list endpoints that touch related objects\n- Transactions & isolation: wrap multi-step writes in a transaction; know READ COMMITTED (Postgres default) vs SERIALIZABLE tradeoffs — stricter isolation trades throughput for correctness on concurrent writes\n- Caching: cache-aside (app checks cache, falls back to DB, populates cache) is the default; write-through when staleness is unacceptable — always pair with a TTL AND an explicit invalidation path (never rely on TTL alone for correctness-critical data)\n- Async processing: push slow/non-critical work (emails, thumbnails, webhooks) onto a queue (SQS/RabbitMQ/Celery/Kafka) — go async when the caller doesn't need the result synchronously or when work needs retry/backoff semantics\n- Testing strategy: unit tests mock external boundaries (DB, HTTP, queue) and cover business logic; integration tests hit a real (test) DB; contract tests (e.g. Pact) verify producer/consumer API compatibility without full E2E\n- Observability: structured JSON logs (not printf strings), a correlation/request ID threaded through every service hop, and distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry) to see cross-service latency — logs alone don't answer \"why is this slow?\"\n- Be ready to reason about the classic system-design archetypes: rate limiter (token bucket vs sliding window, where state lives), URL shortener (hash/base62 + collision handling, read-heavy caching), notification service (fan-out, delivery guarantees, retry/dead-letter queues)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- REST API design\n  - Model resources (nouns), not actions — /orders/{id}/cancel, not /cancelOrder\n  - Use HTTP verbs/status codes correctly: 200/201/204 success, 400 validation, 401/403 auth, 404 missing, 409 conflict, 422 semantic error, 5xx server fault\n- API versioning: URI (/v1/) simplest for public APIs; header/media-type versioning for stricter contract evolution — never break existing clients silently\n- Pagination: cursor-based for large/real-time-changing datasets (stable under inserts/deletes); offset/limit fine for small, static, admin-facing lists\n- Idempotency: PUT/DELETE must be idempotent by definition; for POST (payments, order creation) require an Idempotency-Key header so retries don't double-create\n- Auth patterns\n  - Session + cookie: simplest, server holds state, easy revocation — good default for first-party web apps\n  - JWT: stateless, scales horizontally, but hard to revoke before expiry — keep access tokens short-lived + refresh tokens\n  - OAuth2/OIDC: delegate auth to a provider (Google/Auth0) for third-party or enterprise SSO — don't roll your own\n- ORM vs raw SQL: ORM (SQLAlchemy/Django ORM) for velocity and safety on CRUD-heavy paths; drop to raw SQL/query builder for reporting, bulk ops, or anywhere the ORM generates pathological queries\n- Migrations: every schema change goes through a versioned migration (Alembic/Django migrations), forwards AND backwards compatible during rolling deploy — never hand-edit the prod schema\n- Connection pooling: reuse a bounded pool (pgbouncer, SQLAlchemy pool) sized to DB max_connections / (num app instances) — unbounded connections are a classic outage cause\n- N+1 queries: the #1 ORM footgun — fix with eager loading (select_related/joinedload, prefetch_related/selectinload); watch for it in list endpoints that touch related objects\n- Transactions & isolation: wrap multi-step writes in a transaction; know READ COMMITTED (Postgres default) vs SERIALIZABLE tradeoffs — stricter isolation trades throughput for correctness on concurrent writes\n- Caching: cache-aside (app checks cache, falls back to DB, populates cache) is the default; write-through when staleness is unacceptable — always pair with a TTL AND an explicit invalidation path (never rely on TTL alone for correctness-critical data)\n- Async processing: push slow/non-critical work (emails, thumbnails, webhooks) onto a queue (SQS/RabbitMQ/Celery/Kafka) — go async when the caller doesn't need the result synchronously or when work needs retry/backoff semantics\n- Testing strategy: unit tests mock external boundaries (DB, HTTP, queue) and cover business logic; integration tests hit a real (test) DB; contract tests (e.g. Pact) verify producer/consumer API compatibility without full E2E\n- Observability: structured JSON logs (not printf strings), a correlation/request ID threaded through every service hop, and distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry) to see cross-service latency — logs alone don't answer \"why is this slow?\"\n- Be ready to reason about the classic system-design archetypes: rate limiter (token bucket vs sliding window, where state lives), URL shortener (hash/base62 + collision handling, read-heavy caching), notification service (fan-out, delivery guarantees, retry/dead-letter queues)",
